## Tier reference (manual and CI deploy checks)

**Tier 1 & Tier 2** run inside WSL2 for local testing. Open URLs from the Windows browser at `http://localhost:<port>`.

**Tier 3 (production)** is unchanged: GitLab CI builds the Docker image and deploys via `docker/arm-template.json` to Azure Container Apps. WSL is not part of the production deploy path.

### Tier 1 — Flutter dev (every UI change, WSL)

```bash
flutter pub get && flutter analyze && flutter test && dart format --set-exit-if-changed .
flutter run -d web-server --web-port=3000 --web-hostname=0.0.0.0
# Or: bash scripts/wsl_dev.sh
# Verify http://localhost:3000/
```

### Tier 2 — Docker local smoke (before every push to default, WSL)

```bash
docker compose up --build
# Or: bash scripts/wsl_deploy.sh
# Verify http://localhost:8080/ AND http://localhost:8080/session/s-001 (deep link refresh)
```

### Tier 3 — Azure ARM (production — after push to default branch)

- **Not WSL** — triggered by GitLab CI on default-branch push
- `build_and_deploy` uses `docker/arm-template.json` (do not change for WSL)
- Container App: external HTTPS ingress, `targetPort` 80, startup probe `GET /`
- Confirm pipeline prints: `Deployed to https://{fqdn}`
- Deep-link test: hit `https://{fqdn}/session/s-001` directly (browser refresh) — must NOT 404
- Stage 2 uploads `hackathon-docs/` to blob storage

### Parity rule

What works in Tier 1 must work in Tier 2. Tier 3 uses the **same `docker/Dockerfile` image** built by CI — WSL Tier 2 only validates that image locally before push.
